ARM: dts: qcom: msm8909 APSS watchdog node

CONFIG_QCOM_WDT is already built in, but nothing ever bound to it: msm8909.dtsi
carries no watchdog node, so there is no /dev/watchdog and an unattended board
has no way to recover from a hang on its own. That matters more now that the
product is used headless -- there is no screen to notice a freeze on, and no
one on site to power-cycle.

The block does exist. The vendor device tree, extracted from the recovery
partition, declares:

  qcom,wdt@b017000 {
      compatible = "qcom,msm-watchdog";
      reg = <0xb017000 0x1000>;
      interrupts = <0 3 0  0 4 0>;   /* bark, bite */
      qcom,bark-time = <11000>;
      qcom,pet-time  = <10000>;
  };

Upstream has no node for this family -- msm8916, msm8939 and msm8953 all lack
one -- so this is modelled on qcs404, the closest SoC that does carry it, which
uses the same 0xb017000 window and the same sleep clock. ipq5018 confirms the
bark interrupt convention, GIC_SPI 3 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING, matching the SPI 3
the vendor declares.

The binding has no qcom,apss-wdt-msm8909 entry yet, so the driver matches on
the generic qcom,kpss-wdt fallback. The SoC-specific string is kept first: it
documents the hardware and is what an upstream submission would add to the
binding.

The bark interrupt is optional as far as the driver is concerned
(platform_get_irq_optional), and wiring it buys a pretimeout message in the log
just before the bite resets the board.

This change is inert on its own, by design:

  - CONFIG_WATCHDOG_OPEN_TIMEOUT=0, so nothing arms the watchdog unless
    userspace opens /dev/watchdog;
  - CONFIG_WATCHDOG_HANDLE_BOOT_ENABLED=y, so if the bootloader left it running
    the kernel keeps petting it rather than letting it fire;
  - CONFIG_WATCHDOG_NOWAYOUT is off, so a clean close stops it.

It only exposes the device. Actually arming it -- busybox has the `watchdog`
applet, so no new package is needed -- is a separate decision, because a daemon
that misses its deadline turns a slow box into a rebooting one.

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-msm8909.dtsi b/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-msm8909.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-msm8909.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-msm8909.dtsi
@@ -1529,6 +1529,29 @@
 			};
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * APSS watchdog.  Upstream does not describe it for this family --
+		 * msm8916, msm8939 and msm8953 all lack the node -- but the block is
+		 * there: the vendor DT declares qcom,wdt@b017000 over the same 4 KiB
+		 * window, with bark on SPI 3 and bite on SPI 4.  Modelled on qcs404,
+		 * the closest SoC that does carry the node upstream.
+		 *
+		 * The binding has no qcom,apss-wdt-msm8909 yet, so the driver matches
+		 * on the generic qcom,kpss-wdt fallback; the SoC-specific string
+		 * documents the hardware and is what an upstream submission would add.
+		 *
+		 * The bark interrupt is optional (platform_get_irq_optional), and
+		 * wiring it buys a pretimeout message in the log just before the bite
+		 * resets the board.  On a headless box that line is often the only
+		 * clue left behind by a hang.
+		 */
+		watchdog@b017000 {
+			compatible = "qcom,apss-wdt-msm8909", "qcom,kpss-wdt";
+			reg = <0x0b017000 0x1000>;
+			clocks = <&sleep_clk>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 3 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
+		};
+
